class PipelineConfig:
    def __init__(self, batch_size):
        self._batch_size = None
        self.batch_size = batch_size  # goes through the setter

    @property
    def batch_size(self):
        return self._batch_size

    @batch_size.setter
    def batch_size(self, value):
        if not isinstance(value, int) or value <= 0:
            raise ValueError('batch_size must be a positive integer')
        self._batch_size = value

    @property
    def created_at(self):
        return self._created_at  # no setter -> read-only

The batch_size property validates on every assignment, so invalid values are rejected immediately with a clear message, and callers still use config.batch_size = 5000 naturally. created_at is read-only because it has a getter but no setter, so attempting to assign it raises AttributeError. This keeps validation and immutability concerns inside the object rather than scattered across the codebase.

