To turn a stream into fixed-size batches without loading everything, repeatedly take a slice:

def batched(iterable, n):
    it = iter(iterable)
    while batch := list(islice(it, n)):
        yield batch

Each call to islice pulls up to n items lazily, and the loop stops when a batch comes back empty. This is the standard memory-safe batching pattern for bulk writes (and recent Python provides itertools.batched directly).

followUps.answer:
from itertools import islice

def batched(iterable, size):
    it = iter(iterable)
    while True:
        batch = list(islice(it, size))
        if not batch:
            return
        yield batch

for chunk in batched(stream_of_records(), 1000):
    bulk_insert(chunk)

This pulls at most size items from the source per iteration using islice, which is lazy, so it never reads more than one batch ahead. When islice returns an empty list the source is exhausted and the generator stops. Memory is bounded to one batch regardless of how many millions of records the source yields, which is exactly what you want for streaming bulk inserts: you amortize the per-write overhead by inserting 1,000 rows at a time while keeping the footprint tiny. On recent Python you can simply use itertools.batched(iterable, 1000), which does the same thing; writing it by hand demonstrates you understand the lazy mechanics.
